# The filtered-edge cache is gzip and is the ONE reproducibility-critical data input the runners
# read. Git must never apply line-ending conversion to it (that would corrupt the bytes and break
# byte-reproducibility). Mark all gzip explicitly binary so this never depends on autodetection.
*.gz binary
*.tsv.gz binary

# Normalize Python source to LF in the repo, so a Linux runner and this Windows machine check out
# identical source. Python is newline-agnostic, so this cannot affect a gate verdict; it only keeps
# the tree consistent across the two operating systems.
*.py text eol=lf

# Hash-pinned data files. Their raw bytes are verified against SHA256 constants at runtime
# (coverage_sources.py) and underpin the sacred 20-puzzle corpus invariant. Git must NEVER apply
# line-ending conversion to them: on a Linux checkout (such as the Render build) CRLF->LF
# normalization changes the bytes, the SHA256 no longer matches, and startup aborts with
# "Coverage packs SHA256 mismatch". Marking them binary guarantees byte-identical files on every
# platform, independent of each machine's core.autocrlf setting.
examples/*.json binary
src/spie/questions/brainbloom/data/*.json binary
src/spie/questions/brainbloom/data/*.md binary
